  <section id="purpose" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What the solver does</h2>
    <p>The sketch solver turns a set of points, lines, circles and arcs plus geometric and dimensional constraints into concrete coordinates. It lives in <code>packages/core/src/cad/sketch.ts</code> (522 lines) and is exported from <code>@cadcamfun/core</code> as <code>solveSketch</code>, <code>sketchProfiles</code> and <code>polygonArea</code>. The parametric history resolves its expressions to numbers first, then calls the solver; the solved profiles feed extrudes, revolves and pockets.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>entities: points, lines, circles, arcs</td><td>the same entities with solved coordinates and radii</td></tr>
        <tr><td>constraints: 24 kinds, optionally named</td><td>status, degrees of freedom, free points, redundant equations, conflicts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>options: maxIterations (200), tolerance (1e-9)</td><td>iterations used and the largest residual left</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocViewer3D title="Solved sketch extruded" caption="The fully constrained 40 × 25 rectangle with a Ø10 hole at (20, 12.5), exactly as sketchProfiles returned it (the circle is a 50-point loop at the default 0.01 mm tolerance), extruded 6 mm." solids={[{"kind":"extrude","profile":[[0,0],[40,0],[40,25],[0,25]],"holes":[[[24.961,11.873],[24.843,11.257],[24.649,10.659],[24.382,10.091],[24.045,9.561],[23.645,9.077],[23.187,8.647],[22.679,8.278],[22.129,7.976],[21.545,7.745],[20.937,7.589],[20.314,7.51],[19.686,7.51],[19.063,7.589],[18.455,7.745],[17.871,7.976],[17.321,8.278],[16.813,8.647],[16.355,9.077],[15.955,9.561],[15.618,10.091],[15.351,10.659],[15.157,11.257],[15.039,11.873],[15,12.5],[15.039,13.127],[15.157,13.743],[15.351,14.341],[15.618,14.909],[15.955,15.439],[16.355,15.923],[16.813,16.353],[17.321,16.722],[17.871,17.024],[18.455,17.255],[19.063,17.411],[19.686,17.49],[20.314,17.49],[20.937,17.411],[21.545,17.255],[22.129,17.024],[22.679,16.722],[23.187,16.353],[23.645,15.923],[24.045,15.439],[24.382,14.909],[24.649,14.341],[24.843,13.743],[24.961,13.127],[25,12.5]]],"height":6,"color":"#8aa4c8"}]} labels={[{"text":"a (fixed)","at":[0,0,8],"size":3},{"text":"b","at":[40,0,8],"size":3},{"text":"c","at":[40,25,8],"size":3},{"text":"d","at":[0,25,8],"size":3}]} />
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Data model</h2>
    <p>Points are the unknowns. Every line, circle and arc refers to points by id, so two lines that share an end share the point and move together. A circle adds one more unknown, its radius. An arc is a centre and two points on the circle, counter-clockwise from <code>start</code> to <code>end</code>; its radius is |start − centre| and the solver adds its own equation keeping <code>end</code> on the same circle.</p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Entity and sketch types (sketch.ts)">
{"export interface SketchPoint  { id: string; kind: \u0022point\u0022;  x: number; y: number }\nexport interface SketchLine   { id: string; kind: \u0022line\u0022;   p1: PointId; p2: PointId }\nexport interface SketchCircle { id: string; kind: \u0022circle\u0022; center: PointId; radius: number }\n/** Counter-clockwise from `start` to `end` about `center`. */\nexport interface SketchArc    { id: string; kind: \u0022arc\u0022;    center: PointId; start: PointId; end: PointId }\n\nexport interface Sketch {\n  readonly entities: ReadonlyArray<SketchEntity>\n  readonly constraints: ReadonlyArray<Constraint>\n}"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The unknown vector is laid out as every point&apos;s <code>x</code>, <code>y</code> in entity order, then every circle&apos;s <code>r</code>. A rectangle of four points has 8 unknowns; add a circle with its own centre and it has 11.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="constraints" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Constraint catalogue</h2>
    <p>Each constraint contributes residual equations that are zero when it holds. A constraint can carry an <code>id</code>; without one it is named <code>type#index</code> (for example <code>vertical#2</code>) in conflict reports.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Fields</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Equations</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>fixed</code></td><td>point, x?, y?</td><td>pins a point (either coordinate optional)</td><td>1–2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>coincident</code></td><td>a, b</td><td>two points are one</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>horizontal / vertical</code></td><td>line</td><td>a line is level or plumb</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>horizontalPoints / verticalPoints</code></td><td>a, b</td><td>two points share y / x</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>distance</code></td><td>a, b, value</td><td>point-to-point distance</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>distanceX / distanceY</code></td><td>a, b, value</td><td>signed offset along one axis</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>length</code></td><td>line, value</td><td>line length</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>parallel / perpendicular</code></td><td>a, b</td><td>between two lines</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>equalLength</code></td><td>a, b</td><td>two lines</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>equalRadius</code></td><td>a, b</td><td>two circles or arcs</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>radius / diameter</code></td><td>circle, value</td><td>size of a circle or arc</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>angle</code></td><td>a, b, value</td><td>from line a to line b, counter-clockwise, radians</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pointOnLine / pointOnCircle</code></td><td>point, line | circle</td><td>incidence</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>midpoint</code></td><td>point, line</td><td>point at the middle of a line</td><td>2</td></tr>
